# The server calls no model and holds no model credential. A device sends its model calls to the relay (docs/relay.md), and the
# relay's provider and key (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, or Vertex's settings) are in relay.env (relay.env.example), or in its own
# environment where it runs elsewhere.

# Validated at startup and by `install.sh -s`: the service refuses to run with a
# missing, short or placeholder value (SECURITY.md P3). Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
SESSION_SECRET=change-this-to-a-random-64-char-string

# There is no shared password. Accounts are created from the command line:
#   cd server && npm run cli -- create-user you@example.com
# or, with a mailer configured, people can sign up and recover accounts themselves:
# SPINE_REGISTRATION=open     # default: operator. `open` refuses to start with no mailer.
# SPINE_REGISTRATION=approval # people ask for an account and wait for you: `npm run cli -- approve-user <email>`, or from the account page for the addresses in SPINE_ADMIN_EMAILS. Needs no mailer.
# SPINE_ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com
# SPINE_INSTANCE=private      # label this as a private deployment; refuses `open`
# Plans and payment (docs/billing.md). Off by default; a self-hosted or private instance leaves it off. On, every line below is needed.
# SPINE_BILLING=on
# PADDLE_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox  # sandbox or production, said and never assumed
# PADDLE_API_KEY=
# PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# SPINE_PADDLE_PRICE_YEAR=pri_...
# SPINE_PADDLE_PRICE_MONTH=pri_...
# SPINE_PADDLE_PRICE_FOUNDING=pri_...
# SPINE_PADDLE_PRICE_FAMILY_YEAR=pri_...   # the family plan; both or neither
# SPINE_PADDLE_PRICE_FAMILY_MONTH=pri_...
# SPINE_CHECKOUT_URL=https://pay.spine.yourdomain.com/   # checkout/ served from an origin of its own
# SPINE_WAITLIST=on           # "Tell me when Spine opens" on the front page, while accounts cannot yet be made
# SPINE_WAITLIST_MAX=20000    # most addresses the list holds; past it the form says the list is full
# Mails the instance sends an hour to addresses that are not its accounts (sign-up, resend, reset), and new sign-ups an hour, all sources together:
# SPINE_MAIL_MAX_PER_HOUR=120
# SPINE_SIGNUPS_MAX_PER_HOUR=60
# SPINE_MAILER=brevo          # default: none
# BREVO_API_KEY=
# SPINE_MAIL_SENDER="Account Notices <notices@yourdomain.com>"
# The sender and the subject are visible to the mail provider and in the recipient's
# inbox: your company or product name is fine, a more neutral one reveals less. The links in mail point at
# SPINE_DOMAIN, so that is visible too.

# Where the server keeps accounts, sign-ins, the vault's index and sealed shares. Default: a SQLite file in DATA_DIR. For more than one
# instance, or a host with no disk of its own (Cloud Run), use Postgres: the address carries the password, so keep it out of logs.
# SPINE_STORE=postgres
# SPINE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://spine:password@db.internal:5432/spine?sslmode=require
# SPINE_DATABASE_POOL=10      # connections per instance

# Where the sealed bytes (the vault's objects, a share's copies) are kept. Default: files under DATA_DIR. On a host with no disk of its own
# (Cloud Run) use a bucket made in an EU location, with versioning or soft delete on; the server reaches it as its own Google identity.
# SPINE_OBJECTS=gcs
# SPINE_GCS_BUCKET=spine-sealed-eu
# SPINE_GCS_PREFIX=              # optional: several instances in one bucket

# Your domain, used for share link URLs
SPINE_DOMAIN=spine.yourdomain.com

# Optional. Address(es) of a reverse proxy that is NOT on this host (comma separated); loopback is always trusted. The Docker Compose
# setup sets this to its proxy. The proxy must overwrite X-Forwarded-For with the client's address (SECURITY.md S-31).
# SPINE_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Optional. Who runs this instance, as the privacy notice and terms name them (the controller of everyone's data) and where privacy
# requests go. Unset, they read "the operator of this instance". SPINE_LEGAL_DIR points at your own privacy.md, terms.md and purpose.md
# (see docs/legal/ and docs/compliance/); raise the versions in core/src/legal.ts when you change a text.
# SPINE_OPERATOR_NAME=
# SPINE_OPERATOR_CONTACT=
# SPINE_LEGAL_DIR=

# Optional. Where the source lives. nginx redirects /source, /docs and /security on your domain here,
# so links you publish keep working if the repository moves. Unset: those redirects are left out.
# SPINE_REPO_URL=https://github.com/you/spine

# Optional. Where "Shared securely via Spine" at the foot of a shared page leads: an https address, e.g. the product's landing page.
# Unset, it leads to this instance's own front page (/). `off` leaves the footer as text.
# SPINE_LANDING_URL=https://spine.example.com

# Optional. `on` lets a visitor with no account read a few files of their own in the demo on the front page (default off: every visitor
# then costs model calls). The demo's core is told the limits below; what bounds the cost is the relay's count of demo calls, per address
# and for all visitors together (SPINE_RELAY_DEMO_DAILY_CALLS, SPINE_RELAY_DEMO_TOTAL_DAILY_CALLS in docs/relay.md). Needs a relay.
# SPINE_DEMO_UPLOADS=on
# SPINE_DEMO_MAX_UPLOADS=3
# SPINE_DEMO_MAX_PAGES=5

# Optional. Defaults to <repo>/data wherever the process is started from; both
# systemd units set it explicitly. A relative value resolves against the current
# directory, which is `server/` under npm, so prefer an absolute path.
# DATA_DIR=/var/lib/spine
PORT=3000

# Optional. Fastify binds loopback so nginx is the only public listener; only
# set this if something other than a local reverse proxy must reach it.
# SPINE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Optional. Backups: a daily VACUUM INTO snapshot of the accounts database plus a mirror of
# data/vault/ (sealed objects). The default is beside data/, which protects against
# corruption and mistakes but NOT against losing the disk — point it at
# removable or network storage. `install.sh -s` writes the path into the ReadWritePaths of
# spine-backup.service and spine-web.service (deleting an account removes its backup copy).
# SPINE_BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/backup/spine
# SPINE_BACKUP_KEEP=7
# SPINE_BACKUP_TRASH_DAYS=7

# How long a sign-in lasts: unused for this many hours, or this many days after it began, whichever comes first.
# SPINE_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=24
# SPINE_SESSION_DAYS=14

# Parsing. `text` (the default; `sidecar` is its old name) is the PDF's own text layer, read with PDF.js
# on the device: exact for typed PDFs, nothing for scans, and nothing leaves the host. `ocr` adds
# local Tesseract for pages with no text. `hybrid` sends only the scanned or blank pages to the LLM
# provider as images; `multimodal` sends every page.
# Sending images needs BOTH lines — choosing a parser alone is refused at startup — and
# they go to the relay's provider, so on a deployment that must stay in the EU set the relay
# to Vertex at an eu endpoint (relay.env), not Anthropic.
# SPINE_PARSER=hybrid
# SPINE_MULTIMODAL_ENABLED=true
# SPINE_LLM_MAX_TOKENS_PAGE=16384
#
# `hybrid` tries the cheapest thing first for each page: its own text layer, then local OCR
# (Tesseract), and only then the model. The model sees a page only if it has no text layer
# AND Tesseract is not confident. Runs in the browser (Tesseract.js; English and Romanian packs ship with the web app).
# These are first guesses: watch the "page routed" lines in the browser console and adjust.
# SPINE_OCR=off                 # skip Tesseract: a page with no text layer goes straight to the model. Default on.
# SPINE_OCR_LANGS=ron+eng      # only the packs that ship with the web app (eng, ron) are available on the device
# SPINE_OCR_MIN_CONFIDENCE=85    # mean word confidence, 0-100
# SPINE_OCR_MAX_LOW_SHARE=0.10   # share of words under 60
# SPINE_TEXT_LAYER_MIN_CHARS=50

# Models. Model ids are configuration, never code. The provider, its key and Vertex's endpoint, region and project are the
# relay's (relay.env); what is here is shared: the server passes it to each device, and a relay beside it reads this file too.
# SPINE_LLM_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001   # with a Vertex relay, one Vertex serves where you need it, e.g. gemini-3.8-flash
# Models a person can pick for "Read this page with AI" and "Extract details again", besides SPINE_LLM_MODEL
# (which uploads use and which is always offered first). Comma separated, each `id` or `id=Label`. They all
# run on the relay's provider, endpoint, region and project, and each one must be processed where you
# need it (for the EU: check Google's residency table and Model Garden before adding one).
# SPINE_LLM_MODELS=claude-sonnet-5=Claude Sonnet 5,gemini-3.8-flash=Gemini 3.8 Flash
# SPINE_LLM_MAX_TOKENS_CONTINUATION=1024
# SPINE_LLM_MAX_TOKENS_EXTRACTION=16384
# The only limit on request text is the model's context window, in characters. Longer documents are split, never cut.
# SPINE_LLM_CONTEXT_CHARS=300000
# Model calls a device makes at once (pages read, page pairs checked, documents extracted), 1-16. Providers rate-limit.
# SPINE_LLM_CONCURRENCY=4
# SPINE_LLM_MAX_RETRIES=2
#
# Translating a document into the reader's language sends its extracted text and the owner's notes
# to the relay's provider, once per document and language. `off` stops it: the interface stays translated,
# the content shows in the language it was written in.
# SPINE_TRANSLATION=off
#
# Asking questions of your own records (the Ask page). On by default: each question sends your words and, for the
# documents found for it, their short summary cards, your notes and their results (not the pages) to the relay's provider.
# The relay keeps none of it. Set to off to remove the page.
# SPINE_CHAT=off
#
# Looking for links between documents (a consultation and the tests it asked for, a follow-up), shown as the care chain
# on a document page. On by default: when the data points to a candidate, the short summary cards (not the pages) of the
# document and up to six others go to the relay's provider. Set to off to stop that; links added by hand still work.
# SPINE_LINKING=off
#
# Finding documents by what they mean, and "related documents". Off unless you set this. On, each document's short
# summary card (title, type, provider, summary, doctors, diagnoses and test names, not the pages) is sent to Vertex AI
# to be turned into numbers, and your search words too when you search by meaning. The relay makes the call: Vertex's
# endpoint, region and project for it are in relay.env. `cd server && npm run check-embeddings` sends one test sentence.
# Changing the model or the dimensions starts a new set of vectors (the old ones are removed once the new are complete).
# SPINE_EMBEDDINGS=vertex
# SPINE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-2
# SPINE_EMBEDDING_DIMS=768

# What one account may keep in its vault, in MB: all of it, and one sealed object (docs/crypto-design.md)
# SPINE_VAULT_MAX_MB=5120
# SPINE_VAULT_MAX_OBJECT_MB=100
# How many sealed objects one account may hold (default 50000; the quota above counts bytes, so without this a million one-byte objects fit), and how many
# reads of sealed objects one account, or one source of a share, may have open at once (default 8).
# SPINE_VAULT_MAX_OBJECTS=50000
# SPINE_MAX_CONCURRENT_READS=8
# How much of one share one source may read in an hour, in MB (default 2048): its id is in every address, and its sealed copy can be large.
# SPINE_SHARE_READ_MB_PER_HOUR=2048
# Free space on the data disk that sealed writes must leave, in MB (default 1024; 0 turns it off): below it a save waits, so the accounts database and
# the backup keep room. Say what the disk can bear in SPINE_VAULT_MAX_MB too: ten accounts at the quota should fit.
# SPINE_MIN_FREE_MB=1024
# What one account may keep as sealed share copies, in MB: one object, all live shares together, and how many are live at once
# SPINE_SHARE_MAX_MB=100
# SPINE_SHARE_MAX_TOTAL_MB=1024
# SPINE_SHARE_MAX_LIVE=50

# Optional. A directory of certificates (.pem, .crt, .cer, .der) you trust as authorities for digital signatures: a signer whose
# certificates lead to one is shown as verified (docs/configuration.md).
# SPINE_TRUST_ANCHORS_DIR=

# The relay a device sends model calls to, and the secret (at least 32 random characters) it shares with this server (docs/relay.md).
# Unset, a browser cannot call a model. The relay is a separate service, with its provider and key in relay.env beside this server.
# SPINE_RELAY_URL=https://spine-relay-xxxxx.a.run.app
# SPINE_RELAY_SECRET=
# The relay on this machine instead (docs/relay.md): its own hostname, with SPINE_RELAY_URL=https://<that name>, and its provider key in
# relay.env (relay.env.example). Docker Compose starts its relay service with the profile; `install.sh -r` and `-n` read the name.
# `install.sh -e` writes all four. Another app may hold the relay's port on loopback (8080): SPINE_RELAY_PORT names a free one, and
# `install.sh -r` and `-n` use it for the service and the proxy.
# SPINE_RELAY_PORT=8080
# SPINE_RELAY_DOMAIN=relay.spine.yourdomain.com
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=relay

# The country a share was opened from is read from data/geoip/country.mmdb, which `install.sh -s` downloads and the backup unit refreshes
# daily (server/src/geoip-update.ts). SPINE_GEOIP_DB names a .mmdb country file of your own instead, and nothing is downloaded.
# SPINE_GEOIP_DB=/path/to/country.mmdb
# SPINE_GEOIP_URL=https://github.com/sapics/ip-location-db/releases/download/latest/user-country.mmdb
